875 {\cf20 // A 24 byte string class (can be as small as 17 for x64 and 13 for x86) that can hold strings}\par
876 {\cf20 // with length of up to 23 chars on the stack before going on the heap -}\par
877 {\cf20 // the last byte of the buffer is used for:}\par
878 {\cf20 // - "is small" bit - the highest bit - if "0" then it is small - otherwise its "1" (128)}\par
879 {\cf20 // - if small - capacity left before going on the heap - using the lowest 5 bits}\par
880 {\cf20 // - if small - 2 bits are left unused - the second and third highest ones}\par
881 {\cf20 // - if small - acts as a null terminator if strlen() is 23 (24 including the null terminator)}\par
882 {\cf20 //              and the "is small" bit remains "0" ("as well as the capacity left") so its OK}\par
883 {\cf20 // Idea taken from this lecture about the string implementation of facebook/folly - fbstring}\par
884 {\cf20 // https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kPR8h4-qZdk}\par
885 {\cf20 // TODO:}\par
886 {\cf20 // - optimizations - like not deleting memory unnecessarily in operator= and etc.}\par
887 {\cf20 // - resize/reserve/clear}\par
888 {\cf20 // - replace}\par
889 {\cf20 // - back/front}\par
890 {\cf20 // - iterator stuff}\par
891 {\cf20 // - find & friends}\par
892 {\cf20 // - push_back/pop_back}\par
893 {\cf20 // - assign/insert/erase}\par
894 {\cf20 // - relational operators as free functions - taking const char* as one of the params}\par
